    answer: 'In non-communicative patients, dyspnea is assessed using behavioral observation tools such as the Respiratory Distress Observation Scale (RDOS), which evaluates heart rate, respiratory rate, accessory muscle use, paradoxical breathing pattern, restlessness, grunting at end-expiration, nasal flaring, and fearful facial expression. Management includes opioids (first-line for dyspnea, titrated to respiratory comfort rather than respiratory rate targets), fan or cool air directed at the face (stimulates trigeminal nerve, reducing dyspnea perception), positioning (upright or high Fowler\'s), anxiolytics for associated anxiety, oxygen therapy for hypoxemic patients, and optimizing ventilator settings to reduce work of breathing. Opioids are effective for dyspnea even in patients without pain.',
    difficulty: 2,
    topic: 'End-of-Life & Palliative ICU',
    isFree: false,
