    answer: 'I-PASS is a standardized communication framework for clinical handoffs: (I) Illness severity (stable, "watcher," or unstable), (P) Patient summary (one-liner including diagnosis, pertinent history, and current status), (A) Action list (to-do items with ownership and timeline), (S) Situation awareness and contingency planning ("If X happens, then do Y"), and (S) Synthesis by the receiver (read-back and opportunity for questions). Implementation of I-PASS has been shown to reduce preventable adverse events by 30% and medical errors by 23% without increasing handoff duration. In the ICU, it is particularly important due to the complexity of patients, multiple care transitions per day, and the high-risk nature of critical care decisions.',
