    question: 'What are the key elements of the ventilator-associated event (VAE) surveillance framework?',
    answer: 'The CDC/NHSN VAE framework replaced the older ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) definition with a tiered approach: (1) Ventilator-Associated Condition (VAC): after at least 2 days of stable or decreasing ventilator settings, a sustained increase in daily minimum PEEP of 3 or more cmH2O or FiO2 of 20 or more percentage points for at least 2 calendar days. (2) Infection-related VAC (IVAC): VAC criteria plus abnormal temperature (greater than 38 or less than 36 degrees C) or WBC (greater than 12,000 or less than 4,000) AND a new qualifying antimicrobial started and continued for 4 or more days. (3) Possible/Probable VAP: IVAC plus respiratory specimen with qualifying culture or histopathologic evidence. This framework is more objective and reproducible than prior VAP definitions and captures a broader range of ventilator-associated complications.',
