    question: 'What medication safety practices are essential in the ICU to prevent adverse drug events?',
    answer: 'ICU medication safety practices include: (1) High-alert medication protocols (standardized concentrations, independent double-checks for vasoactive drips, insulin, and heparin, smart pump dose-error reduction systems). (2) Weight-based dosing verified at admission (actual vs. ideal vs. adjusted body weight for appropriate drugs). (3) Renal and hepatic dose adjustment with pharmacist review. (4) Medication reconciliation at all transitions (admission, transfer, discharge). (5) Computerized provider order entry (CPOE) with clinical decision support. (6) Tall-man lettering for look-alike/sound-alike drugs (DOBUTamine vs. DOPamine). (7) Limiting verbal orders to emergencies only. (8) Standardized infusion concentrations to prevent preparation errors. (9) Daily pharmacist participation in ICU rounds, which reduces adverse drug events by 66-78%.',
