    answer: 'Simulation training uses high-fidelity mannequins, task trainers, or standardized patients to replicate clinical scenarios in a safe learning environment. In the ICU, simulation is used for: procedural competency (intubation, central line placement, chest tube insertion, bronchoscopy), crisis management (cardiac arrest, malignant hyperthermia, massive hemorrhage, difficult airway algorithms), team training (CRM principles, communication during emergencies), systems testing (evaluating new protocols, identifying latent safety threats through in-situ simulation), and rare event preparedness (ECMO emergencies, decompensation scenarios). Evidence shows simulation improves procedural success rates, reduces complications, enhances team performance, and identifies system vulnerabilities before they cause patient harm. Effective simulation includes structured debriefing, which is where most learning occurs.',
