    question: 'How should a clinician approach a patient presenting with features of both cardiogenic and distributive (septic) shock?',
    answer: 'Mixed cardiogenic-distributive shock is common in sepsis-induced cardiomyopathy. Assessment requires echocardiography (reduced ejection fraction with LV dilation), hemodynamic monitoring showing high or normal cardiac output with low SVR (septic component) but with evidence of myocardial depression. Management includes: (1) Treat the underlying infection with antibiotics and source control. (2) Judicious fluid resuscitation (avoiding excessive volume in the setting of impaired cardiac function). (3) Norepinephrine as the first-line vasopressor (addresses vasoplegia without excessive tachycardia). (4) Add dobutamine or milrinone if cardiac output remains inadequate despite volume optimization. (5) Consider vasopressin for catecholamine-sparing effects. (6) Use dynamic assessments (passive leg raise, stroke volume variation) rather than static pressures to guide fluid therapy.',
