    question: 'What is targeted temperature management (TTM) after cardiac arrest, and what are the current recommended protocols?',
    answer: 'TTM (formerly therapeutic hypothermia) involves actively controlling body temperature after cardiac arrest to reduce neurological injury. Current evidence (TTM2 trial, 2021) suggests targeting normothermia (less than 37.8 degrees C) is non-inferior to targeting 33 degrees C, shifting practice toward active fever prevention rather than deep cooling. Protocol: initiate temperature control immediately after ROSC, maintain target temperature for at least 24 hours, avoid fever (greater than 37.7 degrees C) for at least 72 hours after ROSC. Cooling methods include surface cooling devices, intravascular cooling catheters, and cold IV fluids (though prehospital cold fluids are no longer recommended due to increased pulmonary edema). Key management during TTM includes continuous temperature monitoring, sedation and shivering control, electrolyte monitoring (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia), and avoiding hyperglycemia.',
