    question: 'How does right ventricular failure complicate hemodynamic management, and what interventions are specifically targeted at RV support?',
    answer: 'RV failure causes systemic venous congestion, reduced LV preload (ventricular interdependence), and low cardiac output. Hemodynamic hallmarks include elevated CVP, low cardiac output, and normal or low PCWP. RV-specific interventions: (1) Optimize preload cautiously (RV is preload-sensitive but easily overdistended; target CVP 8-12 mmHg). (2) Reduce RV afterload with inhaled pulmonary vasodilators (inhaled nitric oxide, inhaled epoprostenol), which selectively dilate the pulmonary vasculature without systemic hypotension. (3) Support RV contractility with dobutamine or milrinone. (4) Maintain systemic perfusion pressure with norepinephrine or vasopressin (coronary perfusion of the RV depends on the aortic-to-RV pressure gradient). (5) Avoid hypoxemia, hypercapnia, and acidosis (all increase PVR). (6) Minimize ventilator-induced increases in PVR by avoiding overdistension and using lung-protective ventilation.',
