    divisionId: ACCS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'What is the concept of ventricular-arterial coupling, and why is it relevant in cardiogenic shock management?',
    answer: 'Ventricular-arterial (VA) coupling describes the relationship between ventricular contractility (end-systolic elastance, Ees) and arterial load (effective arterial elastance, Ea). Optimal coupling occurs when the Ees/Ea ratio is approximately 1.0-1.5, maximizing stroke work efficiency. In cardiogenic shock, Ees is reduced (impaired contractility), and if Ea remains high (due to reflexive vasoconstriction), the Ees/Ea ratio falls well below 1.0, indicating severe uncoupling and inefficient energy transfer. This concept guides therapy: inotropes increase Ees, vasodilators reduce Ea, and mechanical support devices (IABP, Impella) improve coupling by both augmenting contractility and reducing afterload. Monitoring VA coupling helps explain why some patients deteriorate with vasopressors that increase Ea in the setting of already-depressed Ees.',
