    divisionId: ACCS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'What is the role of point-of-care echocardiography in the rapid assessment of undifferentiated shock?',
    answer: 'Focused cardiac ultrasound using protocols such as RUSH (Rapid Ultrasound in Shock and Hypotension) or FATE (Focus Assessed Transthoracic Echo) provides rapid bedside assessment. Key views evaluate: (1) Cardiac function: LV contractility (hyperdynamic vs. depressed), RV size and function (RV dilation suggests PE or pulmonary hypertension). (2) Volume status: IVC diameter and collapsibility (a collapsible IVC less than 2.1 cm suggests fluid responsiveness; a plethoric non-collapsible IVC suggests volume overload or obstructive cause). (3) Pericardium: effusion with or without tamponade physiology (RA/RV diastolic collapse). (4) Aorta: dissection or aneurysm. (5) Pleura: pneumothorax (absent lung sliding), pleural effusion. This focused exam can be completed in under 5 minutes and differentiates cardiogenic, obstructive, hypovolemic, and distributive shock at the bedside without waiting for formal echocardiography.',
