    question: 'What is the pathophysiology and management of vasoplegic syndrome after cardiac surgery?',
    answer: 'Vasoplegic syndrome occurs in 5-25% of post-cardiac surgery patients and is characterized by refractory hypotension with low SVR, normal or elevated cardiac output, and inadequate response to conventional vasopressors. Pathophysiology involves systemic inflammatory response from cardiopulmonary bypass, complement activation, nitric oxide overproduction, vasopressin deficiency, and endothelial dysfunction. Risk factors include preoperative ACE inhibitor/ARB use, prolonged bypass time, and preoperative heart failure. Management: (1) First-line: norepinephrine. (2) Second-line: vasopressin (0.01-0.04 units/min) to address relative vasopressin deficiency. (3) Refractory cases: methylene blue (1-2 mg/kg IV), which inhibits nitric oxide synthase and guanylyl cyclase to restore vascular tone. (4) Hydroxocobalamin (5g IV) as an alternative nitric oxide scavenger. (5) Angiotensin II (Giapreza) for vasodilatory shock refractory to catecholamines.',
