    divisionId: ACCS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'What are the current guidelines for initiating enteral nutrition (EN) in critically ill patients, and why is early EN preferred over parenteral nutrition (PN)?',
    answer: 'Guidelines recommend initiating early enteral nutrition within 24-48 hours of ICU admission in hemodynamically stable patients who cannot maintain volitional intake. EN is preferred over PN because it maintains gut mucosal integrity, reduces bacterial translocation, preserves gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT) function, has lower infection rates, is more cost-effective, and is associated with reduced ICU length of stay. PN is reserved for patients who cannot tolerate EN after 7 days (well-nourished) or 3-4 days (malnourished or at high nutritional risk). Contraindications to early EN include uncontrolled shock requiring escalating vasopressors, uncontrolled hypoxemia/acidosis, bowel ischemia, mechanical bowel obstruction, active upper GI bleeding, and high-output fistula.',
