    question: 'How should life-threatening hyperkalemia be managed in the ICU?',
    answer: 'Hyperkalemia (potassium greater than 6.0 mEq/L or any level with ECG changes) requires emergent, stepwise management: (1) Cardiac membrane stabilization: calcium gluconate 10% 10-20 mL IV over 2-3 minutes (immediate onset, 30-60 minute duration; does not lower potassium but protects the heart from arrhythmias). (2) Intracellular potassium shifting: regular insulin 10 units IV with dextrose 50% 25g (onset 15-30 min), sodium bicarbonate 50-100 mEq IV if acidemic, and nebulized albuterol 10-20 mg (onset 15-30 min, reduces K by 0.5-1.0 mEq/L). (3) Potassium elimination: sodium polystyrene sulfonate (Kayexalate), patiromer, or sodium zirconium cyclosilicate for GI elimination; loop diuretics if renal function permits; and hemodialysis for refractory cases or renal failure. ECG changes progress from peaked T waves to widened QRS to sine wave pattern to ventricular fibrillation.',
