    question: 'What are the metabolic complications of parenteral nutrition, and how are they monitored?',
    answer: 'PN complications include: (1) Hyperglycemia (most common; managed with insulin protocol targeting glucose 140-180 mg/dL). (2) Hypertriglyceridemia (lipid intolerance; monitor triglycerides, hold lipids if greater than 400 mg/dL). (3) Hepatic steatosis and cholestasis (from excessive dextrose calories; manage by cycling PN, reducing dextrose load, and providing some enteral feeding). (4) Refeeding syndrome (hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia). (5) Electrolyte imbalances requiring daily monitoring during initiation. (6) Central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI). (7) Essential fatty acid deficiency (if lipids are withheld for prolonged periods). (8) Metabolic bone disease with long-term use. Monitoring includes daily glucose and electrolytes during initiation, weekly triglycerides and liver function tests, and prealbumin or retinol-binding protein to assess nutritional response (though these are negative acute-phase reactants and must be interpreted cautiously in inflammation).',
