    divisionId: ACCS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'How does thyroid storm present in the ICU, and what is the management approach?',
    answer: 'Thyroid storm is a life-threatening exacerbation of hyperthyroidism with mortality rates of 10-30%. Clinical features include severe tachycardia (often atrial fibrillation), hyperthermia (greater than 40 degrees C), altered mental status (agitation, delirium, coma), GI dysfunction (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, jaundice), and cardiovascular collapse (high-output heart failure). The Burch-Wartofsky score helps diagnose thyroid storm. Management follows a specific sequence: (1) Beta-blockers (propranolol, esmolol) for symptom control and to block peripheral T4-to-T3 conversion. (2) Thionamides (PTU preferred over methimazole because PTU also blocks peripheral conversion) to block new hormone synthesis. (3) Iodine solution (Lugol\'s or SSKI) given at least 1 hour AFTER thionamide to block hormone release (given before thionamide, iodine provides substrate for new hormone synthesis). (4) Corticosteroids (hydrocortisone) to block conversion and treat potential adrenal insufficiency. (5) Supportive care: cooling, fluids, glucose.',
