    question: 'What is the ICU management of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)?',
    answer: 'DKA management follows the principles of fluid, insulin, potassium, and cause: (1) Fluid resuscitation: 0.9% NaCl 1-1.5 L/hr initially, then 250-500 mL/hr with transition to 0.45% NaCl when sodium normalizes. Add dextrose (D5) when glucose falls below 200 mg/dL to allow continued insulin infusion for ketosis resolution. (2) Insulin: regular insulin drip at 0.1-0.14 units/kg/hr (no bolus per current guidelines). Do not stop insulin until the anion gap closes, even if glucose is normal (give dextrose to prevent hypoglycemia). (3) Potassium: check before insulin; if K less than 3.3, hold insulin and replace potassium first; if 3.3-5.3, add 20-40 mEq K to each liter of IV fluid; if greater than 5.3, hold supplementation. (4) Bicarbonate: only if pH less than 6.9. (5) Monitor electrolytes and anion gap every 2-4 hours. (6) Transition to subcutaneous insulin with overlap of 1-2 hours before stopping the drip.',
