    divisionId: ACCS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'What is tumor lysis syndrome (TLS), and how does it cause respiratory failure?',
    answer: 'TLS occurs when rapid destruction of tumor cells (spontaneously or after chemotherapy) releases intracellular contents: potassium, phosphate, uric acid, and nucleic acids. Diagnostic criteria (Cairo-Bishop) include two or more of: hyperuricemia, hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, and hypocalcemia (from calcium-phosphate precipitation). Respiratory failure in TLS occurs through multiple mechanisms: (1) Fluid overload from aggressive IV hydration used for prevention/treatment. (2) Acute kidney injury from uric acid crystal nephropathy and calcium-phosphate deposition, leading to uremia and pulmonary edema. (3) Hyperkalemia-induced cardiac arrhythmias compromising hemodynamics. (4) Severe metabolic acidosis stimulating compensatory hyperventilation and respiratory muscle fatigue. Prevention: rasburicase (recombinant urate oxidase) for high-risk patients, aggressive hydration, allopurinol for intermediate risk. Treatment: correct electrolytes, dialysis for refractory hyperkalemia, hyperphosphatemia, or AKI.',
