    divisionId: ACCS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'A 58-year-old post-CABG patient develops acute hypotension, tachycardia, elevated CVP, and muffled heart sounds on postoperative day 2. Ventilator shows decreased tidal volume delivery on pressure-targeted mode. What is the most likely diagnosis and immediate management?',
    answer: 'This presentation is classic for cardiac tamponade (Beck\'s triad: hypotension, elevated JVP/CVP, muffled heart sounds) with pulsus paradoxus. The decreased tidal volume delivery on pressure-targeted ventilation results from reduced cardiac output and impaired venous return. Post-surgical tamponade can present without classic pericardial effusion due to loculated clot. Immediate management: (1) Emergent bedside echocardiography to confirm (diastolic chamber collapse, IVC plethora, respiratory variation in mitral/tricuspid inflow). (2) Volume resuscitation to maintain filling pressures. (3) Avoid positive pressure ventilation increases that further impair venous return. (4) Emergent pericardiocentesis or surgical re-exploration (preferred post-cardiac surgery due to clotted blood). (5) Vasopressors for hemodynamic support as a bridge. Do not delay definitive drainage for diagnostic confirmation if the patient is hemodynamically unstable.',
