    question: 'A trauma patient with bilateral pulmonary contusions and a left-sided flail chest is on volume-control ventilation. Despite FiO2 1.0 and PEEP 16, PaO2 is 55 mmHg. The patient is sedated and paralyzed. What additional interventions should be considered?',
    answer: 'With refractory hypoxemia despite maximal conventional settings, escalation is needed: (1) Prone positioning: first-line for severe ARDS (P/F less than 150); however, assess for contraindications in trauma (unstable spine, open abdomen, pelvic fixator). If proning is feasible, sessions of at least 16 hours are recommended. (2) Inhaled pulmonary vasodilators: inhaled nitric oxide (starting at 20 ppm) or inhaled epoprostenol to improve V/Q matching in ventilated lung regions. (3) Recruitment maneuver if derecruitment is suspected, though use cautiously with pulmonary contusions (risk of worsening hemorrhage). (4) Assess for concurrent hemothorax requiring drainage. (5) Consider independent lung ventilation if injury is predominantly unilateral. (6) VV-ECMO if hypoxemia remains refractory (P/F less than 80) and the injury is potentially reversible. (7) Optimize hemoglobin to improve oxygen-carrying capacity (transfuse if Hb less than 7 g/dL).',
