    divisionId: ACCS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'A patient on day 10 of mechanical ventilation for ARDS has improved oxygenation (P/F 220 on PEEP 8, FiO2 0.40) but fails every SBT with tachypnea and diaphoresis within 15 minutes. What systematic evaluation should be performed?',
    answer: 'Systematic evaluation of SBT failure includes: (1) Cardiac: obtain BNP/NT-proBNP before and after SBT (a rise suggests weaning-induced cardiac dysfunction from increased preload and afterload); consider echo and diuresis trial. (2) Respiratory: check rapid shallow breathing index (f/Vt greater than 105 predicts failure), assess for auto-PEEP, evaluate secretion burden and airway patency, consider cuff leak test for laryngeal edema. (3) Neurological/muscular: assess MIP (should be stronger than -20 cmH2O), perform diaphragm ultrasound, screen for ICU-acquired weakness (MRC score less than 48/60), evaluate for delirium. (4) Metabolic: correct hypophosphatemia, hypomagnesemia, hypothyroidism, and adrenal insufficiency. (5) Psychological: address anxiety with reassurance and graduated SBT approach. (6) Nutritional: ensure adequate protein and caloric intake. (7) Consider a tracheostomy for prolonged weaning (facilitates secretion management, reduces dead space, improves patient comfort).',
