    divisionId: ACCS_DIVISION_ID,
    question: 'A 30-year-old pregnant patient (32 weeks gestation) presents with severe pre-eclampsia, respiratory distress, and SpO2 88% on 15L non-rebreather. CXR shows bilateral infiltrates. What are the key considerations for managing respiratory failure in this patient?',
    answer: 'Key considerations: (1) Differentiate the cause: pulmonary edema (most common in severe pre-eclampsia from capillary leak, volume overload, and low oncotic pressure), aspiration, peripartum cardiomyopathy, or ARDS. (2) Airway management: pregnant patients are at higher aspiration risk (decreased lower esophageal sphincter tone, delayed gastric emptying); perform rapid sequence intubation with cricoid pressure, use a smaller ETT (6.5-7.0 due to edematous airways), and expect a difficult airway (Mallampati score worsens in pregnancy). (3) Ventilation targets: PaCO2 28-32 mmHg (normal for pregnancy), PaO2 greater than 70 mmHg for fetal oxygenation. (4) Positioning: left lateral tilt to prevent aortocaval compression. (5) Definitive treatment: delivery of the fetus is the only cure for pre-eclampsia; magnesium sulfate for seizure prophylaxis. (6) Fluid management: restrict fluids (pre-eclamptic patients are particularly susceptible to pulmonary edema). (7) Coordinate with obstetrics for emergent delivery if maternal or fetal condition worsens.',
